{
  "raw": {
    "https://georgetown.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/Georgetown_Admin_Careers/job/Main-Campus/Director-of-the-Center-for-Student-Engagement_JR27315": {
      "title": "Director of the Center for Student Engagement",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-04\nFull time\n\nGeorgetown University comprises two unique campuses in the nation’s capital. With the Hilltop Campus located in the heart of the historic Georgetown neighborhood, and the Capitol Campus, just minutes from the U.S. Capitol and U.S. Supreme Court, Georgetown University offers rigorous academic programs, a global perspective, and unparalleled opportunities to engage with Washington, D.C. Our community is a close-knit group of remarkable individuals driven by intellectual inquiry, a commitment to social justice, and a shared dedication to making a difference in the world. Requirements Job Overview The Director of the Center for Student Engagement promotes student involvement and furthers community on campus while linking academic life with co-curricular engagement.   This position represents student co-curricular involvement as an institutional priority in a variety of contexts, supervises the access to, and allocation of student organization benefits, works with faculty and student advisory boards and advises students.  The Director provides strategic leadership and management of a department of nine full-time program staff members, three part-time graduate assistants, and several student staff members. Professional staff on this team provide advising, program development and administrative support to over 250 student groups. Content areas include Student Organizations, Outdoor Education, Debate, New Student Orientation, Student Media, Student Government, Club Sports, the Georgetown Program Board, transition programs, and campus leadership programs.  The Director is responsible for overseeing budgets, operations, administrative management, and assessment of a wide variety of student-engagement programs. The Director convenes groups of peers and staff across the university to coordinate and standardize student engagement strategies across schools, departments and programs, in both curricular and co-curricular units.  This person is expected to set and uphold exemplary standards of practice for advising students, developing student leadership values and skills, innovating programs and services, and engaging a wide-variety of stakeholders in the support for co-curricular learning.  Work Interactions This position reports to the Senior Associate Vice President for Student Engagement as part of the Community and Identity Formation cluster within the Division of Student Affairs.  Others who report to the Senior Associate Vice President are the Executive Directors of the Center for Social Justice and the Academic Resource Center, the Director of the Office of Student Conduct, the Senior Director of Student Formation, and the Director of the Military and Veteran’s Resource Center.  The Director of the Center for Student Engagement attends the Student Affairs Directors’ meetings.  All student activities-related functions are reviewed by this position.   Directly reporting to this position are the Associate Director of Student Engagement, the Director of Outdoor Education, the Director of Orientation, Transition, and Family Engagement, and the Director of Policy Debate.  The Director of the Center for Student Engagement must represent student co-curricular involvement as an institutional priority in a variety of contexts, ensuring that campus planning and policy support a strong campus life program that provides significant opportunity for overall growth and student leadership development.  The Director is responsible directly for over 250 student organizations, and indirectly for approximately 100 more reporting to other departments in the university. The Director is responsible for establishing and implementing policy for all student organizations regarding events and projects on and off campus, seeking always to maintain co-curricular involvement as a learning experience for students.  The Director is responsible for anticipating, advising, and participating in the management of the institution’s risk and liability in student programming and its commitment to speech and expression.  This person manages a staff with diverse areas of involvement and expertise; therefore, the Director must remain current on issues across a wide variety of content areas.  The Director is responsible for managing a fast paced, unpredictable environment while maintaining an educational focus.  The Director must establish clear standard procedures and policies and also develop a staff with maximum ability to exercise independent thinking and judgment.  The Director is responsible for the professional development of the staff.  This position is subject to extended work hours during peak weeks at high volume periods of the semester.  Requirements and Qualifications Requirements:  Master’s Degree in Higher Education/Student Affairs, human development, counseling or related discipline Minimum of 10 years of full-time, professional experience working with student organizations, student programs, college student leadership development or related functional area Familiarity with contemporary understanding of post-secondary student formation, development, and growth, particularly with regard to intersectional identity development and the value of on-campus engagement Familiarity and facility working with students as an advisor, supervisor, mentor, supporter, and educator  Ability to work both independently and collaboratively with a wide variety of stakeholders on a broad range of projects  Strong organizational and communication skills    Preferred qualifications:  Advanced or terminal degree in a discipline related to student development, leadership development, human development, or higher education; a terminal degree may substitute for three years of professional work experience Understanding of the mission, vision, and context of Jesuit and Catholic higher education, particularly with regard to contextual influence on student formation   Pay Range: The projected salary or hourly pay range for this position which represents the full range of anticipated compensation is: $66,783.00 - $126,720.23 Compensation is determined by a number of factors including, but not limited to, the candidate’s individual qualifications, experience, education, skills, and certifications, as well as the University’s business needs and external factors. Current Georgetown Employees: If you currently work at Georgetown University, please exit this website and login to GMS ( gms.georgetown.edu ) using your Net ID and password. Then select the Career worklet on your GMS Home dashboard to view Jobs at Georgetown. Submission Guidelines: Please note that in order to be considered an applicant for any position at Georgetown University you must submit a resume for each position of interest for which you believe you are qualified. Documents are not kept on file for future positions. Need Assistance: If you are a qualified individual with a disability and need a reasonable accommodations for any part of the application and hiring process, please visit the Office for Equal Opportunity Compliance website for general information about requesting accommodations , as well as information about requesting accommodations specifically for applicants . You also can also contact the Office for Equal Opportunity Compliance at (202) 687-4798 or oeoc@georgetown.edu . Need some assistance with the application process? Please call 202-687-2500. For more information about the suite of benefits, professional development and community involvement opportunities that make up Georgetown's commitment to its employees, please visit the Georgetown Works website . EEO Statement: GU is an Equal Opportunity Employer . All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply, and will receive consideration for employment without regard to age, citizenship, color, disability, family responsibilities, gender identity and expression, genetic information, marital status, matriculation, national origin, race, religion, personal appearance, political affiliation, sex, sexual orientation, veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by law . Benefits: Georgetown University offers a comprehensive and competitive benefit package that includes medical, dental, vision, disability and life insurance, retirement savings, tuition assistance, work-life balance benefits, employee discounts and an array of voluntary insurance options. You can learn more about benefits and eligibility on the Department of Human Resources website ."
    },
    "https://georgetown.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/Georgetown_Admin_Careers/job/Capitol-Campus-55-H-Street-Residence-NW-1st-Floor/Administrative-Assistant--Center-for-Faith-and-Justice---McCourt-School-of-Public-Policy----Georgetown-University_JR27328": {
      "title": "Administrative Assistant, Center for Faith and Justice - McCourt School of Public Policy -  Georgetown University",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-04\nFull time\n\nGeorgetown University comprises two unique campuses in the nation’s capital. With the Hilltop Campus located in the heart of the historic Georgetown neighborhood, and the Capitol Campus, just minutes from the U.S. Capitol and U.S. Supreme Court, Georgetown University offers rigorous academic programs, a global perspective, and unparalleled opportunities to engage with Washington, D.C. Our community is a close-knit group of remarkable individuals driven by intellectual inquiry, a commitment to social justice, and a shared dedication to making a difference in the world. Requirements Job Overview The Administrative Assistant for the Center on Faith and Justice, McCourt School of Public Policy, Georgetown University, will provide high-level administrative support to the Director of the Center on Faith and Justice and assist with the daily operations of the Center. The ideal candidate will be organized, proactive, and skilled in managing logistics, scheduling, and communications. This role is essential to ensuring the smooth functioning of the Center’s activities and the Director’s commitments. The Administrative Assistant provides executive, administrative, and analytical support to the Director in a wide variety of areas including: special projects, teaching and course administration, correspondence, maintenance of the Director’s calendar; planning, coordinating, and executing high-profile events on behalf of the Director; developing, supporting, and maintaining administrative support processes; working closely with, and providing support for, other Center staff; and problem solving for the day-to-day operations of the Center on Faith and Justice. The Administrative Assistant will coordinate activities on behalf of the Director, serve as liaison to senior management across campus, assume primary responsibility for administrative support for the Center, identify issues or problems, prioritize tasks effectively, and work independently with a high level of confidentiality.  The Administrative Assistant will also be responsible for assisting with event planning and logistics—both on content as well as administratively. Additional duties include, but are not limited to: Maintain and  manage the Director’s calendar, including meetings, speaking engagements and travel arrangements. Draft, review and manage emails, letters and other communication on behalf of the Director. Assist in preparing materials and logistics for speaking events, interviews and other public appearances. Oversee the day-to-day administrative operations, including ordering supplies, managing mail and maintaining office systems. Provide administrative assistance to other staff members as required and coordinate office supply and facilities requests.  Handle reimbursements for the Center. Act as the primary point of contact for inquiries directed to the Director and the Center.  Assist in updating and maintaining the Center’s digital platform with relevant content. Work Interactions This position reports to the Executive Director of the Center on Faith and Justice, and serves as liaison with University administration, University Deans, senior management, faculty, staff, students, alumni, industry partners and others in the Georgetown community. The position works closely with, and provides support for other staff at the Center on Faith and Justice. The position will oversee the revenue and expenses related to certain projects and events that are funded through RGAs. This position will oversee 1-2 student workers. Requirements and Qualifications Bachelor’s degree or equivalent experience in a related field. 0-2 years proven experience in administrative roles, preferably in nonprofit or mission-driven organizations or equivalent combination of education and work experience. Exceptional organizational and multitasking abilities with a keen attention to detail. Strong written and verbal communication skills. Proficiency in office software (Google Workspace) and scheduling tools. Experience in event planning and logistics coordination is a plus. Discretion and professionalism in handling confidential information. Interest in or commitment to the mission of the Center on Faith and Justice. Proactive and self-motivated with a problem-solving mindset. Ability to work collaboratively in a fast-paced environment. Strong interpersonal skills with a positive and flexible attitude. A passion for social justice, faith-based initiatives, and the public discourse surrounding these issues is desirable. Experience with social media is preferred. Familiarity with issues at the intersection of faith, justice, religion, politics, and policy. Work Mode Designation This position has been designated as Hybrid. Please note that work mode designations are regularly reviewed in order to meet the evolving needs of the University.  Such review may necessitate a change to a position’s mode of work designation. Complete details about Georgetown University’s mode of work designations for staff positions can be found on the Department of Human Resources website: https://hr.georgetown.edu/mode-of-work-designation . Pay Range: The projected salary or hourly pay range for this position which represents the full range of anticipated compensation is: $44,022.00 - $73,406.80 Compensation is determined by a number of factors including, but not limited to, the candidate’s individual qualifications, experience, education, skills, and certifications, as well as the University’s business needs and external factors. Current Georgetown Employees: If you currently work at Georgetown University, please exit this website and login to GMS ( gms.georgetown.edu ) using your Net ID and password. Then select the Career worklet on your GMS Home dashboard to view Jobs at Georgetown. Submission Guidelines: Please note that in order to be considered an applicant for any position at Georgetown University you must submit a resume for each position of interest for which you believe you are qualified. Documents are not kept on file for future positions. Need Assistance: If you are a qualified individual with a disability and need a reasonable accommodations for any part of the application and hiring process, please visit the Office for Equal Opportunity Compliance website for general information about requesting accommodations , as well as information about requesting accommodations specifically for applicants . You also can also contact the Office for Equal Opportunity Compliance at (202) 687-4798 or oeoc@georgetown.edu . Need some assistance with the application process? Please call 202-687-2500. For more information about the suite of benefits, professional development and community involvement opportunities that make up Georgetown's commitment to its employees, please visit the Georgetown Works website . EEO Statement: GU is an Equal Opportunity Employer . All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply, and will receive consideration for employment without regard to age, citizenship, color, disability, family responsibilities, gender identity and expression, genetic information, marital status, matriculation, national origin, race, religion, personal appearance, political affiliation, sex, sexual orientation, veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by law . Benefits: Georgetown University offers a comprehensive and competitive benefit package that includes medical, dental, vision, disability and life insurance, retirement savings, tuition assistance, work-life balance benefits, employee discounts and an array of voluntary insurance options. You can learn more about benefits and eligibility on the Department of Human Resources website ."
    },
    "https://georgetown.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/Georgetown_Admin_Careers/job/Medical-Center/Clinical-Research-Coordinator-II_JR25086": {
      "title": "Clinical Research Coordinator II",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-03\nFull time\n\nGeorgetown University comprises two unique campuses in the nation’s capital. With the Hilltop Campus located in the heart of the historic Georgetown neighborhood, and the Capitol Campus, just minutes from the U.S. Capitol and U.S. Supreme Court, Georgetown University offers rigorous academic programs, a global perspective, and unparalleled opportunities to engage with Washington, D.C. Our community is a close-knit group of remarkable individuals driven by intellectual inquiry, a commitment to social justice, and a shared dedication to making a difference in the world. Requirements Job Overview The Clinical Research Coordinator II (CRC II) is a lead coordinator on multiple surgical services (General Surgery, Burn, Breast, Endocrine, Trauma, Colorectal, and Surgical Oncology) and possibly transplant clinical research studies, under the oversight of the Principal Investigator (PI) and direct supervision of the Assistant Research Manager in collaboration with a Research Director.  The CRC II is responsible for the implementation of their assigned portfolio of clinical studies, including regulatory submissions, data abstraction/entry/management, participant screening and enrollment, and study management.  Given the role is an intermediate level position, the coordinator will manage moderate to high-level complexity studies.  The role involves supporting the research on-call rotation amongst the research team to support surgical and transplant services and their enrollments.  Work Interactions The Clinical Research Coordinator II works under the direct supervision of the Assistant Research Manager in collaboration with the Research Director and conducts studies under direct oversight of the Principal Investigators (PIs).  This individual will interact directly with patients, research participants, and their families.  They work within the multidisciplinary team of the healthcare system and the university medical center, including the clinical research office, sponsored programs financial office, research development services, respective grants managers, and serve as primary point of contact with the sponsors, CROs, and IRBs.   Requirements and Qualifications Bachelor's degree in a scientific or related field with at least 3 years CRC I or equivalent clinical research experience.  An equivalent combination of education and experience may be substituted. Working knowledge of the federal regulations and federal agencies including the Food and Drug Administration (FDA) and the Office for Human Research Protections (OHRP), as well as familiarity with the International Conference on Harmonisation Guideline for Good Clinical Practice (ICH GCP). Strong organizational, critical thinking, and problem-solving skills. Working knowledge of clinical trials contracting and budgeting, including understanding of Medicare Coverage Analysis (MCA) and clinical trials billing compliance, both industry and non-industry. Excellent demonstrated interpersonal, verbal, and written communication skills. Strong proficiency in Microsoft, Google, data capture systems, electronic medical records, and other computer applications. Preferred Qualifications Experience with OnCore (Clinical Trials Management System). Certified clinical research certification (SoCRA or ACRP) preferred and is expected within one year of eligibility. Work Mode Designation This position has been designated as On-Campus. Please note that work mode designations are regularly reviewed in order to meet the evolving needs of the University.  Such a review may necessitate a change to a position’s mode of work designation. Complete details about Georgetown University’s mode of work designations for staff and AAP positions can be found on the Department of Human Resources website: https://hr.georgetown.edu/mode-of-work-designation . Pay Range: The projected salary or hourly pay range for this position which represents the full range of anticipated compensation is: $21.16 - $35.29 Compensation is determined by a number of factors including, but not limited to, the candidate’s individual qualifications, experience, education, skills, and certifications, as well as the University’s business needs and external factors. Current Georgetown Employees: If you currently work at Georgetown University, please exit this website and login to GMS ( gms.georgetown.edu ) using your Net ID and password. Then select the Career worklet on your GMS Home dashboard to view Jobs at Georgetown. Submission Guidelines: Please note that in order to be considered an applicant for any position at Georgetown University you must submit a resume for each position of interest for which you believe you are qualified. Documents are not kept on file for future positions. Need Assistance: If you are a qualified individual with a disability and need a reasonable accommodations for any part of the application and hiring process, please visit the Office for Equal Opportunity Compliance website for general information about requesting accommodations , as well as information about requesting accommodations specifically for applicants . You also can also contact the Office for Equal Opportunity Compliance at (202) 687-4798 or oeoc@georgetown.edu . Need some assistance with the application process? Please call 202-687-2500. For more information about the suite of benefits, professional development and community involvement opportunities that make up Georgetown's commitment to its employees, please visit the Georgetown Works website . EEO Statement: GU is an Equal Opportunity Employer . All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply, and will receive consideration for employment without regard to age, citizenship, color, disability, family responsibilities, gender identity and expression, genetic information, marital status, matriculation, national origin, race, religion, personal appearance, political affiliation, sex, sexual orientation, veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by law . Benefits: Georgetown University offers a comprehensive and competitive benefit package that includes medical, dental, vision, disability and life insurance, retirement savings, tuition assistance, work-life balance benefits, employee discounts and an array of voluntary insurance options. You can learn more about benefits and eligibility on the Department of Human Resources website ."
