    "https://georgetown.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/Georgetown_Admin_Careers/job/Main-Campus/Electrical-Engineer_JR26334": {
      "title": "Electrical Engineer",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-07-08\nFull time\n\nGeorgetown University comprises two unique campuses in the nation’s capital. With the Hilltop Campus located in the heart of the historic Georgetown neighborhood, and the Capitol Campus, just minutes from the U.S. Capitol and U.S. Supreme Court, Georgetown University offers rigorous academic programs, a global perspective, and unparalleled opportunities to engage with Washington, D.C. Our community is a close-knit group of remarkable individuals driven by intellectual inquiry, a commitment to social justice, and a shared dedication to making a difference in the world. Requirements Job Overview The Electrical Engineer, Planning and Facilities Management / Office of Engineering and Utilities provides engineering analysis, recommendation, and subject matter expertise services on multi-discipline building projects of small to large scope, manages electrical engineering projects and programs, and serves a as technical liaison to the Georgetown Energy Partnership.  They will ensure proper management and oversight of the facilities and electrical systems and relevant partnerships for reliable & cost-effective maintenance and upkeep of electrical. They will direct electrical deferred maintenance priorities and lead electrical programs which have complex features, from the concept stage through the development of construction documents or program implementation. They shall develop engineering solutions and implement necessary programs for corrective action, energy efficiency, and code compliance. The position will also perform special project activities requiring application of technical expertise to the development of solutions to unusually difficult problems as assigned. Additional duties include, but are not limited to: Lead and support project development and perform design/construction project reviews for compliance with GU Standards and incorporation of best practices for electrical engineer and energy efficiency. Identify and correct deficiencies to optimize medium voltage and low voltage electrical system performance and ensure individual system elements are well-maintained. Train and inform maintenance staff on best practices regarding electrical system design and maintenance. Develop, enhance, and track preventive maintenance to support electrical system performance, energy efficiency, and safety. Assess building inventory and associated systems to develop capital plans to correct deficiencies and oversee the implementation of discipline specific projects and programs Create and maintain design standards and specifications related to electrical and other divisions as relevant. Work Interactions This position works with the Office of Engineering and Utilities, Capital Projects, Facilitates Operations, Georgetown Energy Partnership, and building end-users to complete tasks. The position shall be directed by the Engineering and Utilities Leadership to complete studies, support projects, implement new process and procedures, and investigate issues with the support of the engineering team. The position shall work with Facilities Maintenance as a consultative resource to solve issues and improve operations while providing lateral direction to improve system performance within electrical, fleet management, and other relevant activities. The position shall work with Capital Projects to review project plans to ensure electrical considerations are met and design standards are included in the design and specifications. The position will also work with end-users to inform them of potential changes and resolve issues related to electrical and other related disciplines. The position will frequently interact with the Georgetown Energy Partnership to coordinate operations, review and approve project scopes, and support overall utilities delivery and capital improvements. Requirements and Qualifications Bachelor’s degree in electrical engineering, or related (applicable) discipline and/or equivalent combination Knowledge of electrical distribution and generation systems . The ability to direct and guide a group or team through task completion to attain goals Strong listener that can tactfully express ideas or opinions to resolve issues and maintain professional working relationships in difficult situations related to budget and scope priorities on campus. Excellent analytical thinking, problem-solving abilities, and ability to produce results independently. A high attention to detail and accuracy Knowledge of engineering economic evaluation methods to conduct cost benefit analysis prioritizing program measures and total project budgets Familiar with design & construction guidelines creating standards for relevant disciplines Qualifications 3+ years of progressive experience as an engineer in operations, design, maintenance, or construction Licensed as a Engineer-in-Training (EIT) or Professional Engineer (PE). Preference for individuals registered as a CEM (Certified Energy Manager), ASHRAE Building Commissioning Professional Certification (BCxP), Certified Commissioning Professional (CCP), Accreditation as a LEED (Leadership in Energy & Environmental Design) Green Associate, LEED Accredited Professional Experience with emergency power, building low voltage systems, campus distribution systems, program management, project engineering, project management. Work Mode Designation This position has been designated as On-Campus. Please note that work mode designations are regularly reviewed in order to meet the evolving needs of the University.  Such review may necessitate a change to a position’s mode of work designation. Complete details about Georgetown University’s mode of work designations for staff positions can be found on the Department of Human Resources website: https://hr.georgetown.edu/mode-of-work-designation . Pay Range: The projected salary or hourly pay range for this position which represents the full range of anticipated compensation is: $66,783.00 - $126,720.23 Compensation is determined by a number of factors including, but not limited to, the candidate’s individual qualifications, experience, education, skills, and certifications, as well as the University’s business needs and external factors. Current Georgetown Employees: If you currently work at Georgetown University, please exit this website and login to GMS ( gms.georgetown.edu ) using your Net ID and password. Then select the Career worklet on your GMS Home dashboard to view Jobs at Georgetown. Submission Guidelines: Please note that in order to be considered an applicant for any position at Georgetown University you must submit a resume for each position of interest for which you believe you are qualified. Documents are not kept on file for future positions. Need Assistance: If you are a qualified individual with a disability and need a reasonable accommodations for any part of the application and hiring process, please visit the Office for Equal Opportunity Compliance website for general information about requesting accommodations , as well as information about requesting accommodations specifically for applicants . You also can also contact the Office for Equal Opportunity Compliance at (202) 687-4798 or oeoc@georgetown.edu . Need some assistance with the application process? Please call 202-687-2500. For more information about the suite of benefits, professional development and community involvement opportunities that make up Georgetown's commitment to its employees, please visit the Georgetown Works website . EEO Statement: GU is an Equal Opportunity Employer . All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply, and will receive consideration for employment without regard to age, citizenship, color, disability, family responsibilities, gender identity and expression, genetic information, marital status, matriculation, national origin, race, religion, personal appearance, political affiliation, sex, sexual orientation, veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by law . Benefits: Georgetown University offers a comprehensive and competitive benefit package that includes medical, dental, vision, disability and life insurance, retirement savings, tuition assistance, work-life balance benefits, employee discounts and an array of voluntary insurance options. You can learn more about benefits and eligibility on the Department of Human Resources website ."
    },
    "https://georgetown.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/Georgetown_Admin_Careers/job/Main-Campus/Locksmith-and-Safe-Repairer_JR26298": {
      "title": "Locksmith and Safe Repairer",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-07-02\nFull time\n\nGeorgetown University comprises two unique campuses in the nation’s capital. With the Hilltop Campus located in the heart of the historic Georgetown neighborhood, and the Capitol Campus, just minutes from the U.S. Capitol and U.S. Supreme Court, Georgetown University offers rigorous academic programs, a global perspective, and unparalleled opportunities to engage with Washington, D.C. Our community is a close-knit group of remarkable individuals driven by intellectual inquiry, a commitment to social justice, and a shared dedication to making a difference in the world. Requirements Job Overview Position: # P13052  Full-time: 40 hours Pay: $27.50 Location: 37th & O Street NW Washington, DC 20057 Provides skilled locksmith services to support the University community as part of Facilities Management. Responsibilities include the installation, replacement, and repair of door locks and related hardware, as well as cutting keys, rekeying, and picking locks as needed. Performs journey-level maintenance and repairs in accordance with industry and trade standards. Work is completed in response to service requests, scheduled preventive maintenance inspections, unscheduled or special projects, and the performance of all duties as assigned. Incumbents utilize standard locksmith tools, equipment, materials, and supplies while consistently following all safety rules, regulations, and precautions. Work Interactions Reporting to the Supervisor of Lock Shop and Access Control, the Locksmith I works closely with the Lock Shop Foreperson, fellow Locksmiths, and all supporting Maintenance Services shops within Planning and Facilities Management. The incumbent also collaborates with all stakeholders across the University as needed to complete assigned tasks. The work performed directly supports the University’s mission by ensuring a safe, secure, and well-maintained environment in which the campus community can live, learn, and work. Requirements and Qualifications Minimum Qualifications At least 1–2 years of experience in locksmithing or a closely related trade. High school diploma or GED. Completion of apprenticeship or equivalent training in the locksmith trade.  Must be bondable. Basic understanding of locksmith terminology, tools, materials, and safe work practices. Introductory knowledge of reading and interpreting blueprints, plans, and hardware schedules. Familiarity with applicable codes and standards, including ADA, NFPA, and ANSI/BHMA. Skilled in pinning locks, changing cores, cutting keys, and performing safe lock and hardware repairs. Experience using a Computerized Maintenance Management System (CMMS) to track work orders, service requests, and maintenance activities. Familiarity with preventive maintenance programs for door hardware and locks. Strong communication, organizational, and recordkeeping abilities, with the capacity to collaborate effectively with team members and stakeholders to troubleshoot and resolve locksmith- and door-related issues. Ability to safely perform work at heights using ladders or scaffolding and lift up to 50 lbs. Ability to work independently and teams. Ability to work all shifts, including weekends, holidays, and during emergency or weather-related operations. Must possess and maintain a valid driver’s license. Preferred Qualifications Additional experience in locksmithing or related trades beyond the minimum requirement. Certification from a recognized locksmithing program or professional organization (e.g., ALOA, Associated Locksmiths of America), . Experience working in a higher education, healthcare, large campus environment, or government. Experience with electronic access control systems, keypads, and card readers. Advanced knowledge of keying systems, master keying, and high-security locks. Strong problem-solving skills with the ability to handle complex lock and door hardware issues independently. Knowledge of sustainability and security best practices in building maintenance. Proficiency with Microsoft Office Suite, Google Workspace, and/or other software used for reporting, recordkeeping, and workflow management. Work Mode Designation This position has been designated as On-Campus. Please note that work mode designations are regularly reviewed in order to meet the evolving needs of the University.  Such review may necessitate a change to a position’s mode of work designation. Complete details about Georgetown University’s mode of work designations for staff and AAP positions can be found on the Department of Human Resources website: https://hr.georgetown.edu/mode-of-work-designation . Current Georgetown Employees: If you currently work at Georgetown University, please exit this website and login to GMS ( gms.georgetown.edu ) using your Net ID and password. Then select the Career worklet on your GMS Home dashboard to view Jobs at Georgetown. Submission Guidelines: Please note that in order to be considered an applicant for any position at Georgetown University you must submit a resume for each position of interest for which you believe you are qualified. Documents are not kept on file for future positions. Need Assistance: If you are a qualified individual with a disability and need a reasonable accommodations for any part of the application and hiring process, please visit the Office for Equal Opportunity Compliance website for general information about requesting accommodations , as well as information about requesting accommodations specifically for applicants . You also can also contact the Office for Equal Opportunity Compliance at (202) 687-4798 or oeoc@georgetown.edu . Need some assistance with the application process? Please call 202-687-2500. For more information about the suite of benefits, professional development and community involvement opportunities that make up Georgetown's commitment to its employees, please visit the Georgetown Works website . EEO Statement: GU is an Equal Opportunity Employer . All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply, and will receive consideration for employment without regard to age, citizenship, color, disability, family responsibilities, gender identity and expression, genetic information, marital status, matriculation, national origin, race, religion, personal appearance, political affiliation, sex, sexual orientation, veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by law . Benefits: Georgetown University offers a comprehensive and competitive benefit package that includes medical, dental, vision, disability and life insurance, retirement savings, tuition assistance, work-life balance benefits, employee discounts and an array of voluntary insurance options. You can learn more about benefits and eligibility on the Department of Human Resources website ."
    },
    "https://georgetown.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/Georgetown_Admin_Careers/job/Law-Center/Special-Events-Worker-II_JR26165": {
      "title": "Special Events Worker II",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-06-22\nFull time\n\nGeorgetown University comprises two unique campuses in the nation’s capital. With the Hilltop Campus located in the heart of the historic Georgetown neighborhood, and the Capitol Campus, just minutes from the U.S. Capitol and U.S. Supreme Court, Georgetown University offers rigorous academic programs, a global perspective, and unparalleled opportunities to engage with Washington, D.C. Our community is a close-knit group of remarkable individuals driven by intellectual inquiry, a commitment to social justice, and a shared dedication to making a difference in the world. Requirements Position: # P137057 Full-time: 40 hours Pay: $18.87 Location: 37th & O Street NW Washington, DC 20057 Job Overview Working in accordance with a detailed plan to support academic, social, and cultural events, the incumbent performs general and specific cleaning, deliveries, moving furniture, set ups and break downs of equipment, such as tables, chairs, podiums, stages and coat racks. In emergency or unusual situations, the incumbent performs other tasks as assigned including but not limited to minor carpentry work, painting, moving, custodial responsibilities of floor care, classroom cleaning, restroom cleaning, snow and ice removal and clean up associated with water or fire damages. Incumbent ensures that work complies with all applicable rules and regulations. Uses and disposes of materials in accordance with applicable environmental safety and control regulations. Keeps abreast of changes and new developments in applicable codes and regulations. The incumbent must dress in proper uniform and carry valid University identification at all times. The position is designated, “Emergency Employee” by the University’s definition. Emergency employees must report to work regardless of weather conditions. Work Interactions The incumbent will interact mainly with fellow special event workers but may interact occasionally with the event’s coordinator and the Office of Student Activity Facilities staff. The incumbent must work cooperatively with all of these individuals so that the work can be completed timely and efficiently and that events can go smoothly. Requirements and Qualifications High school diploma or equivalency. Prefer one year of custodial work experience and event set up. Driver’s license from Maryland, District of Columbia, or Virginia is required. Must be qualified to conform to the University Driving Policy. Must be able to read and understand product label instructions and to read and understand University policies and procedures. Must be able to work well as part of a team and independently. Must be capable of relating custodial and maintenance problems clearly to supervisor and/or the Work Management Center. This position is considered an emergency position that requires that incumbent report to work during inclement weather at the regularly scheduled time regardless of whether the University is closed, opening late or closing early. Must be able to be flexible to accommodate emergency situations such as staffing shortages and snow removal. Must be able to lift up to fifty pounds, push and/or pull equipment up to 100 pounds on a rolling dolly. Must be able to use arms and hands to lift and carry up to thirty-five pounds and tools of the trade. Must be able to walk, climb stairs and ladders, bend and crouch, to reach and pull and to work on equipment. Must be able to see, hear, smell and have a fundamental understanding of chemicals. Work is performed inside a building and in outside spaces during all seasons and in all types of weather. Employee may be exposed to potentially dangerous and/or hazardous conditions and is required to know and use appropriate and acceptable safety measures. Assigned hours will be dependent upon the needs of the University. Work Mode Designation This position has been designated as On-Campus. Please note that work mode designations are regularly reviewed in order to meet the evolving needs of the University. Such review may necessitate a change to a position’s mode of work designation. Complete details about Georgetown University’s mode of work designations for staff positions can be found on the Department of Human Resources website: https://hr.georgetown.edu/mode-of-work-designation . Current Georgetown Employees: If you currently work at Georgetown University, please exit this website and login to GMS ( gms.georgetown.edu ) using your Net ID and password. Then select the Career worklet on your GMS Home dashboard to view Jobs at Georgetown. Submission Guidelines: Please note that in order to be considered an applicant for any position at Georgetown University you must submit a resume for each position of interest for which you believe you are qualified. Documents are not kept on file for future positions. Need Assistance: If you are a qualified individual with a disability and need a reasonable accommodations for any part of the application and hiring process, please visit the Office for Equal Opportunity Compliance website for general information about requesting accommodations , as well as information about requesting accommodations specifically for applicants . You also can also contact the Office for Equal Opportunity Compliance at (202) 687-4798 or oeoc@georgetown.edu . Need some assistance with the application process? Please call 202-687-2500. For more information about the suite of benefits, professional development and community involvement opportunities that make up Georgetown's commitment to its employees, please visit the Georgetown Works website . EEO Statement: GU is an Equal Opportunity Employer . All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply, and will receive consideration for employment without regard to age, citizenship, color, disability, family responsibilities, gender identity and expression, genetic information, marital status, matriculation, national origin, race, religion, personal appearance, political affiliation, sex, sexual orientation, veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by law . Benefits: Georgetown University offers a comprehensive and competitive benefit package that includes medical, dental, vision, disability and life insurance, retirement savings, tuition assistance, work-life balance benefits, employee discounts and an array of voluntary insurance options. You can learn more about benefits and eligibility on the Department of Human Resources website ."
