    "https://georgetown.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/Georgetown_Admin_Careers/job/Medical-Center/Research-Assistant-III_JR27713": {
      "title": "Research Assistant III",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-30\nFull time\n\nGeorgetown University comprises two unique campuses in the nation’s capital. With the Hilltop Campus located in the heart of the historic Georgetown neighborhood, and the Capitol Campus, just minutes from the U.S. Capitol and U.S. Supreme Court, Georgetown University offers rigorous academic programs, a global perspective, and unparalleled opportunities to engage with Washington, D.C. Our community is a close-knit group of remarkable individuals driven by intellectual inquiry, a commitment to social justice, and a shared dedication to making a difference in the world. Requirements Job Overview We are looking to hire a Research Assistant III (Laboratory Technician) within the Department of Microbiology and Immunology, Georgetown University Medical Center, School of Medicine – Georgetown University. This position is critical to the successful implementation of grant-funded studies in the woodchuck animal model, and as such, it is expected that the applicant has prior laboratory experience and is familiar with routine assays and methods, such as ELISA and quantitative PCR. Together with the PI, he/she coordinates and manages woodchuck-specific sample processing and then performs all assays/methods independently, including the determinations of viral titers and immune responses in blood, liver and other tissues of woodchucks. The individual is responsible for the handling and processing of stat and archived samples. He/she eventually trains and supervises Undergraduate and Graduate students during internships in relevant sample processing and laboratory assays. This position also involves shipment of samples to diagnostic or collaborating laboratories. Additional duties include, but are not limited to: Processing of woodchuck samples (blood, serum, liver, other tissues) Quantitative viral assays (woodchuck hepatitis virus (WHV) and hepatitis B virus (HBV)), including slot blots for viral DNA, Southern and Northern hybridization for viral DNA and RNA, and real time PCR for viral DNA and RNA Host immune response assays, including proliferation assays for T-cells, ELISpot assays for IFN-gamma secreting T-cells, FACS-based degranulation assays for cytotoxic T lymphocytes, real time PCR for immune markers (cytokines and leukocyte surface markers) in whole blood lysates and liver biopsies ELISAs for viral proteins or antibodies against these proteins Organization of sample records and data from the above assays in standard spread sheets Work Interactions The Department of Microbiology and Immunology is dedicated to the study of infectious diseases caused by diverse pathogens, including basic research on and treatment of HBV in the woodchuck animal model. The individual is a team member of the PI’s group and critical for the sample processing and analysis from ongoing and future woodchuck studies. He/she reports directly to the PI. He/she interacts with other members of the PI’s group to coordinate sample processing and analysis. He/she interacts further with Undergraduate and Graduate students concerning sample processing and analysis. Requirements and Qualifications Bachelor’s degree or higher with prior laboratory experience in general for several years (2-5 years). A preference will be given to individuals with prior experience with viral and host assays for HBV or other viruses Prior experience in performing sample processing and analysis under supervision and/or independently Must be willing and able to acquire skills and experience with woodchuck sample-specific assays Note that some of the above assays may require university certification for direct handling and conducting assays with radioisotopes (32P). Incumbent must be able to follow establish standardized protocols and procedures for routine use in the laboratory, as applied to woodchuck samples (i.e., most assays are lab-generated and cannot be purchased commercially) Must be able to perform new protocols and procedures like the above assays including applications of new reagents, and to use appropriate methodologies to validate results Must be able to conduct analysis to ensure correct and precise specificity of signals against appropriate standards and other controls The candidate is expected to ensure that high quality results are achieved in a timely and organized manner and that data obtained can meet the scientific objectives of the study. Work Mode Designation This position has been designated as On-Campus. Please note that work mode designations are regularly reviewed in order to meet the evolving needs of the University. Such review may necessitate a change to a position’s mode of work designation. Complete details about Georgetown University’s mode of work designations for staff positions can be found on the Department of Human Resources website: https://hr.georgetown.edu/mode-of-work-designation . Pay Range: The projected salary or hourly pay range for this position which represents the full range of anticipated compensation is: $18.40 - $27.33 Compensation is determined by a number of factors including, but not limited to, the candidate’s individual qualifications, experience, education, skills, and certifications, as well as the University’s business needs and external factors. Current Georgetown Employees: If you currently work at Georgetown University, please exit this website and login to GMS ( gms.georgetown.edu ) using your Net ID and password. Then select the Career worklet on your GMS Home dashboard to view Jobs at Georgetown. Submission Guidelines: Please note that in order to be considered an applicant for any position at Georgetown University you must submit a resume for each position of interest for which you believe you are qualified. Documents are not kept on file for future positions. Need Assistance: If you are a qualified individual with a disability and need a reasonable accommodations for any part of the application and hiring process, please visit the Office for Equal Opportunity Compliance website for general information about requesting accommodations , as well as information about requesting accommodations specifically for applicants . You also can also contact the Office for Equal Opportunity Compliance at (202) 687-4798 or oeoc@georgetown.edu . Need some assistance with the application process? Please call 202-687-2500. For more information about the suite of benefits, professional development and community involvement opportunities that make up Georgetown's commitment to its employees, please visit the Georgetown Works website . EEO Statement: GU is an Equal Opportunity Employer . All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply, and will receive consideration for employment without regard to age, citizenship, color, disability, family responsibilities, gender identity and expression, genetic information, marital status, matriculation, national origin, race, religion, personal appearance, political affiliation, sex, sexual orientation, veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by law . Benefits: Georgetown University offers a comprehensive and competitive benefit package that includes medical, dental, vision, disability and life insurance, retirement savings, tuition assistance, work-life balance benefits, employee discounts and an array of voluntary insurance options. You can learn more about benefits and eligibility on the Department of Human Resources website ."
    },
    "https://georgetown.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/Georgetown_Admin_Careers/job/St-Marys-4th-Floor/Director-of-Community-Engagement-and-Nursing-Workforce-Development_JR27707": {
      "title": "Director of Community Engagement and Nursing Workforce Development",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-30\nFull time\n\nGeorgetown University comprises two unique campuses in the nation’s capital. With the Hilltop Campus located in the heart of the historic Georgetown neighborhood, and the Capitol Campus, just minutes from the U.S. Capitol and U.S. Supreme Court, Georgetown University offers rigorous academic programs, a global perspective, and unparalleled opportunities to engage with Washington, D.C. Our community is a close-knit group of remarkable individuals driven by intellectual inquiry, a commitment to social justice, and a shared dedication to making a difference in the world. Requirements Job Overview The Director of Community Engagement and Nursing Workforce Development provides strategic leadership in cultivating, expanding, and sustaining relationships with clinicians and professional networks to advance the education mission and workforce development priorities of the Georgetown University Berkley School of Nursing (BSON). Grounded in Jesuit values of cura personalis , service, and social justice, this role advances BSON’s mission by fostering meaningful partnerships that support clinical learning, student success, mentorship, experiential learning, and the future of nursing. Through intentional outreach and engagement with clinicians, administrators, community members and other relevant healthcare leaders, the Director strengthens the connection between academic nursing and practice while advancing BSON’s national reputation in nursing education, nursing science, and workforce innovation. The Director will design and implement a comprehensive short- and long-term engagement strategy that integrates clinical preceptor opportunities, student mentorship, career networking, and pipeline initiatives, with particular attention to expanding access for students from communities underrepresented in nursing. This position plays a critical role in expanding high-quality clinical and experiential learning opportunities, by cultivating clinicians who serve as preceptors, mentors, professional advisors, and champions of nursing education. The Director will also develop strategic pipeline initiatives that increase visibility of nursing as a profession and introduce students to nursing science, health, and healthcare careers through partnerships with  schools, and regional organizations. Through health-focused learning experiences, nursing exploration activities, boot camps, and immersion programs, the Director will strengthen BSON’s role as a committed partner in the region while inspiring future generations to pursue careers in healthcare.   Work Interactions The Director collaborates extensively across internal and external stakeholders, including: Dean, Associate/Assistant Deans Directors, Faculty and Staff within BSON Clinicians and professional networks Clinical Placements, to hand off cultivated relationships for formal affiliation agreements and placement scheduling Marketing & Communications, to align on public-facing storytelling, event promotion, and CRM/Salesforce reporting tied to engagement outcomes Community partners interested in development of career pathways Other Georgetown University teams and offices, as needed to support cross-institutional initiatives and community partnerships The role requires a high degree of cross-functional coordination and the ability to represent BSON in academic, community, and professional settings.   Requirements and Qualifications Master’s degree in nursing, healthcare administration, education, or a related field required. Minimum of three to five years of progressive experience in nursing (practice or education), strategic partnerships, workforce development, relationship cultivation, public engagement, civic engagement, outreach, external relations, or related areas. Demonstrated ability to engage diverse constituencies across academic and civic-related environments. Strong verbal and written communication, presentation, relationship-building, interpersonal, project management, and organizational skills. Ability to develop, implement, and evaluate strategic initiatives using measurable outcomes and key performance indicators (KPIs). Experience using relationship management systems, databases, or CRM platforms (e.g., Salesforce) to track engagement activities and outcomes. Demonstrated commitment to student success, professional formation, nursing workforce development, and advancing the visibility of nursing as a profession.  Ability to analyze data and translate insights into strategic action  Commitment to Jesuit mission and values, including equity, inclusion, and social impact  Preferred Experience Experience supporting clinical education, preceptor engagement, mentorship initiatives, or experiential learning programs. Experience within a school of nursing or academic health environment  Experience in recruiting and managing volunteers  Familiarity with workforce development in nursing education  Work Mode Designation This position has been designated as Hybrid requiring 2-3 days on campus. Regular on-site engagement is expected to support events, student programming, and stakeholder collaboration. Evening and occasional weekend hours may be required to support alumni and community events. Please note that work mode designations are regularly reviewed in order to meet the evolving needs of the University.  Such review may necessitate a change to a position’s mode of work designation. Complete details about Georgetown University’s mode of work designations for staff positions can be found on the Department of Human Resources website: https://hr.georgetown.edu/mode-of-work-designation . Pay Range: The projected salary or hourly pay range for this position which represents the full range of anticipated compensation is: $54,616.00 - $100,493.33 Compensation is determined by a number of factors including, but not limited to, the candidate’s individual qualifications, experience, education, skills, and certifications, as well as the University’s business needs and external factors. Current Georgetown Employees: If you currently work at Georgetown University, please exit this website and login to GMS ( gms.georgetown.edu ) using your Net ID and password. Then select the Career worklet on your GMS Home dashboard to view Jobs at Georgetown. Submission Guidelines: Please note that in order to be considered an applicant for any position at Georgetown University you must submit a resume for each position of interest for which you believe you are qualified. Documents are not kept on file for future positions. Need Assistance: If you are a qualified individual with a disability and need a reasonable accommodations for any part of the application and hiring process, please visit the Office for Equal Opportunity Compliance website for general information about requesting accommodations , as well as information about requesting accommodations specifically for applicants . You also can also contact the Office for Equal Opportunity Compliance at (202) 687-4798 or oeoc@georgetown.edu . Need some assistance with the application process? Please call 202-687-2500. For more information about the suite of benefits, professional development and community involvement opportunities that make up Georgetown's commitment to its employees, please visit the Georgetown Works website . EEO Statement: GU is an Equal Opportunity Employer . All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply, and will receive consideration for employment without regard to age, citizenship, color, disability, family responsibilities, gender identity and expression, genetic information, marital status, matriculation, national origin, race, religion, personal appearance, political affiliation, sex, sexual orientation, veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by law . Benefits: Georgetown University offers a comprehensive and competitive benefit package that includes medical, dental, vision, disability and life insurance, retirement savings, tuition assistance, work-life balance benefits, employee discounts and an array of voluntary insurance options. You can learn more about benefits and eligibility on the Department of Human Resources website ."
