    "https://georgetown.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/Georgetown_Admin_Careers/job/Medical-Center/Project-Manager--ARPA-H-funded-project-consortium--Georgetown-University-Medical-Center_JR27858": {
      "title": "Project Manager, ARPA-H funded project/consortium, Georgetown University Medical Center",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-15\nFull time\n\nGeorgetown University comprises two unique campuses in the nation’s capital. With the Hilltop Campus located in the heart of the historic Georgetown neighborhood, and the Capitol Campus, just minutes from the U.S. Capitol and U.S. Supreme Court, Georgetown University offers rigorous academic programs, a global perspective, and unparalleled opportunities to engage with Washington, D.C. Our community is a close-knit group of remarkable individuals driven by intellectual inquiry, a commitment to social justice, and a shared dedication to making a difference in the world. Requirements Job Overview The Project Manager oversees ARPA-H projects at Georgetown University Medical Center (GUMC). The Project Manager is the administrative lead for SEEDS (Sequence-to-disease, Environment-aware Ensembles of Disordered proteins at Scale), a multi-institution consortium funded by the Advanced Research Projects Agency for Health (ARPA-H) under an Other Transaction Agreement and co-led by Georgetown University and the Buck Institute for Research on Aging. Reporting directly to the Lead Principal Investigator, the incumbent runs the business of the program: coordinating a consortium of academic, clinical, and industry performers; gathering and assembling reports and deliverables from across the team; tracking budgets and milestone-based payments; building business models and making fast decisions on operational and administrative matters. The Project Manager is also a strategic partner to the Lead PI, helping set priorities and plans, anticipating risks and opportunities across the consortium, and driving the execution of project strategy. ARPA-H programs move at a fast, milestone-driven cadence, and this position calls for someone who is highly organized, nimble, and able to keep many parallel workstreams moving on tight timelines. Additional duties include, but are not limited to:  Coordinate, and sustain the multi-institution consortium, including onboarding performers, coordinating sub-agreements with university contracts offices, and running the consortium's operational and meeting structure. Set reporting calendars, gather reports from teams at all performer sites, and assemble and quality-check progress reports, milestone packages, and other deliverables for PI review and timely submission to ARPA-H. Track program budgets, spending, and milestone-based payments across project team partners, and ensure funds are allocated and distributed according to the approved program budget. Partner with the Lead PI on consortium project strategy, priorities, and planning; prepare briefing materials; and drive follow-through on decisions and action items. Sustain academic and industry partner relationships. Serve as the primary administrative point of contact for partner institutions, and university offices, and coordinate consortium communications and events. These functions are performed in accordance with Georgetown University Medical Center's philosophy, policies, procedures, and standards.   Work Interactions The Project Manager (PM) works at the center of a large, fast-moving, multi-institution program. The PM interacts daily with the Lead PI and program leadership at Georgetown. The PM also works regularly with principal investigators, administrators, and staff at academic, clinical, and industry performer sites; with ARPA-H program and contracting staff where required; and with Georgetown offices, including the Office of Sponsored Programs, Office of Technology and Commercialization, contracts, departmental finance, and communications with nimble execution. This position reports directly to the Lead Principal Investigator of the SEEDS program. The PM performs these duties under the oversight of the Lead PI, exercising independent judgment on routine operational matters and bringing program-level decisions to the Lead PI for approval. The position does not currently have direct reports, but provides day-to-day administrative coordination to project staff and site administrators across participating institutions. Through meticulous project stewardship and collaborative engagement, the Project Manager directly supports Georgetown University’s mission to advance groundbreaking medical discoveries, foster ethical translational research, and serve the common good through transformative healthcare solutions. Overall position oversight is by the GUMC Vice President for Research.   Requirements and Qualifications Master’s degree in biology, neuroscience, biochemistry, pharmacology, or a closely related scientific discipline. (In lieu of a Master's degree, a minimum of 10 years of experience within the biomedical industry or supporting federally funded research will be considered). Minimum of 5 years of scientific program and/or project management experience, preferably within the biomedical industry or directly supporting federally funded scientific research. Proven track record of managing complex scientific programs, translational research projects, or multi-center initiatives. Demonstrated understanding of therapeutic development processes across preclinical and early translational stages. Strong familiarity with regulatory, ethical, and compliance frameworks governing federally funded research and medical device/therapeutic development. Proficiency with Microsoft Office Suite, project management platforms, and electronic grant management software. Proficiency in effectively adopting AI for project management. Preferred Qualifications Ph.D. in a biomedical science field or PMP (Project Management Professional) certification. Direct experience managing large federal grants or programs (ARPA-H, DARPA, NIH, or DoD). Specialized familiarity with Neurodegeneration and Cancer (multiscale experimental and/or computational approaches)   Work Mode Designation This position has been designated as Hybrid. Please note that work mode designations are regularly reviewed in order to meet the evolving needs of the University.  Such review may necessitate a change to a position’s mode of work designation. Complete details about Georgetown University’s mode of work designations for staff positions can be found on the Department of Human Resources website: https://hr.georgetown.edu/mode-of-work-designation .   Pay Range: The projected salary or hourly pay range for this position which represents the full range of anticipated compensation is: $80,429.00 - $157,238.93 Compensation is determined by a number of factors including, but not limited to, the candidate’s individual qualifications, experience, education, skills, and certifications, as well as the University’s business needs and external factors. Current Georgetown Employees: If you currently work at Georgetown University, please exit this website and login to GMS ( gms.georgetown.edu ) using your Net ID and password. Then select the Career worklet on your GMS Home dashboard to view Jobs at Georgetown. Submission Guidelines: Please note that in order to be considered an applicant for any position at Georgetown University you must submit a resume for each position of interest for which you believe you are qualified. Documents are not kept on file for future positions. Need Assistance: If you are a qualified individual with a disability and need a reasonable accommodations for any part of the application and hiring process, please visit the Office for Equal Opportunity Compliance website for general information about requesting accommodations , as well as information about requesting accommodations specifically for applicants . You also can also contact the Office for Equal Opportunity Compliance at (202) 687-4798 or oeoc@georgetown.edu . Need some assistance with the application process? Please call 202-687-2500. For more information about the suite of benefits, professional development and community involvement opportunities that make up Georgetown's commitment to its employees, please visit the Georgetown Works website . EEO Statement: GU is an Equal Opportunity Employer . All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply, and will receive consideration for employment without regard to age, citizenship, color, disability, family responsibilities, gender identity and expression, genetic information, marital status, matriculation, national origin, race, religion, personal appearance, political affiliation, sex, sexual orientation, veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by law . Benefits: Georgetown University offers a comprehensive and competitive benefit package that includes medical, dental, vision, disability and life insurance, retirement savings, tuition assistance, work-life balance benefits, employee discounts and an array of voluntary insurance options. You can learn more about benefits and eligibility on the Department of Human Resources website ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195975": {
      "title": "Tenure-Line Professor (Open Rank), Gracias Family Chair in Security and Emerging Technology",
      "description": "Department: Georgetown University: Main Campus: Walsh School of Foreign Service Location: Main Campus Posted: Oct 20, 2026 The Walsh School of Foreign Service (SFS) at Georgetown University invites applications for an open-rank, tenure-line faculty position (Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor) as the Gracias Family Chair in Security and Emerging Technology. We seek a scholar whose research, policy, and teaching interests address the critical intersections of national/international security and the emerging technology landscape including but not limited to AI, quantum computing, biotechnology, autonomous systems, or advanced materials. The successful candidate will demonstrate a record or promise of outstanding scholarly achievement, policy relevance, innovative teaching, and institutional service. Key Responsibilities Research: Maintain an active, high-impact research agenda producing peer-reviewed publications and policy-relevant scholarship at the intersection of security studies, AI, or other emerging technologies. Teaching: Deliver undergraduate and graduate courses in the Walsh School of Foreign Service on topics directly related to the position and in broader support of the Security Studies Program and the School of Foreign Service. Service: Participation in departmental and school governance, mentorship of undergraduate and graduate students and engagement with university centers, policy institutes, and the broader Washington, D.C. security and technology policy ecosystem. Salary Ranges In accordance with Georgetown University guidelines and rank structures, the full-time 9-month base salary ranges for this tenure-line position are: Assistant Professor: $101,165 – $115,000 Associate Professor: $128,985 – $146,702 Full Professor: $181,119 – $209,878 Salary and title are commensurate with academic rank, credentials, and experience. Required: Ph.D. or equivalent terminal degree in Political Science, International Relations, Public Policy, Law, Science and Technology Studies, or Computer Science/Engineering with a policy orientation. Candidates with an appropriate focus from other fields are welcome to apply. Expertise: Demonstrated expertise in the security implications of artificial intelligence and emerging technologies. Applicants should submit the following materials through Interfolio: Cover Letter outlining academic background, research agenda, and teaching philosophy. Curriculum Vitae (CV). Writing Sample(s) (up to two articles, chapters, or working papers). Teaching Evaluations or a teaching portfolio (if available). Those applying at the Assistant level should submit 3 letters of recommendation. If applying at the Associate or Full level, please provide the names and contact information of 3-4 references. Application System Questions: Direct to Shelby Reese, Director of Faculty Affairs smr251@georgetown.edu Position & Search Questions: Direct to Professor Mark Giordano mark.giordano@georgetown.edu Review of applications will begin on November 20, 2026. The window for submissions will remain open until the position is filled."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195976": {
      "title": "American Druze Foundation Postdoctoral Fellowship",
      "description": "Department: Georgetown University: Main Campus: Walsh School of Foreign Service Location: Main Campus Posted: Oct 20, 2026 Deadline: Jan 04, 2027 The Georgetown University Center for Contemporary Arab Studies (CCAS) is pleased to partner with the American Druze Foundation (ADF) to offer the ADF Fellowship in Druze and Arab Studies at CCAS. The purpose of the ADF Fellowship is to promote research on the Druze and Arab minorities with a concentration in the political, economic, and social history of the Druze. The ADF Fellowship supports academic research in the disciplines of history, political science, sociology, economics, anthropology, and archaeology. It cannot be used to support research that relates essentially to matters theological or religious in scope and nature. The ADF Fellowship is available to both untenured and tenured holders of PhDs. The fellowship residence at Georgetown University is required for a period of nine months in the AY 2027-2028 For those who hold a tenured or tenure-track job, the program will match 50% of the fellow’s base salary for the academic year at their university (up to a maximum of $70,000 for the year). For those who do not hold a tenured or tenure-track job, the program will offer a fellowship of $70,000, with an additional $5,000 in research funds. During the academic year, the Fellow must work on research and publications related to Druze and Arab studies. The Fellow is encouraged to deliver a public lecture on a relevant topic to be determined with the Director of the CCAS, and must design and teach for CCAS one graduate seminar on the Druze and the broader topics of socio-cultural pluralism, identity politics, and subnational communities in the Arab world. Finally, the Fellow must be in residence at the CCAS for the duration of the academic year. The fellowship will be awarded to a doctoral graduate (Ph.D.) from an accredited university or granting institution. The fellowship is based on an international competition. Applicants must have a record of relevant research in the disciplines of history, political science, sociology, economics, anthropology, and archaeology. Applicants will be assessed on the originality of their scholarship and the high quality of their academic record. Cover letter Curriculum Vitae (CV) Please provide the names and contact information of two professional references. If you are shortlisted for the fellowship, you will be notified by the end of January and asked to submit two recommendation letters. The letters should be submitted directly by the recommenders (via the Interfolio application system) within two weeks of notification. Course proposal for seminar 500 word research / publication proposal Writing sample (dissertation chapter, book chapter, or article) The application deadline for the 2026-2027 ADF Fellowship is January 4, 2027. Questions about the fellowship may be directed to Ms. Vicki Valosik at ve44@georgetown.edu . For information on the current ADF Fellow, click here."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/181306": {
      "title": "Program Director, Psychiatric-Mental Health Nurse Practitioner (PMHNP), Associate Professor or Professor on the Educator Track, BSON - Georgetown University Medical Center",
      "description": "Department: Georgetown University: Medical Center: School of Nursing Location: St. Mary's 4th Floor Posted: Feb 04, 2026 Deadline: Feb 28, 2027 Georgetown University Berkley School of Nursing (BSON) is seeking an innovative and experienced educator to lead the PMHNP Post-graduate Certificate Program. The PMHNP educator will serve as specialty track Director and will be responsible for the oversight, management, implementation and quality of the PMHNP track of the Advanced Practice Registered Nurse (APRN) Program and will report directly to the Assistant Dean for APRN Programs. The PMHNP director oversees the team to carry out all matters relating to the curriculum, course development, course delivery, course quality and evaluation (including course and faculty evaluation). The PMHNP faculty will work collaboratively with the other specialty track faculty in all matter’s curriculum, including clinical placement oversight. We seek a mission-driven educator with strong clinical expertise, a commitment to advancing health equity, and a deep appreciation for the Jesuit values that animate our work. This faculty position is held on the clinical educator track (non-tenure) in the School of Nursing at Associate or Full Professor rank with scholarship, teaching and service expectations with the following time commitments: ● Teaching: 40-50 % (8-9 credits) ● Student clinical oversight, including placement: 40% ● Scholarship: 5-10% ● Service: 5-10 % Minimum requirements ● Terminal degree (research focused, practice focused e.g., DNP, PhD, EdD) ● Holds certification as a Psychiatric Mental Health Nurse Practitioner (PMHNP) ● Eligible for an unencumbered RN license in the District of Columbia ● Minimum of 3-5 years progressive leadership or administrative experience at the undergraduate and/or graduate levels ● Prior experience with curriculum development, evaluation, and outcomes ● Experience in developing course content and teaching in a variety of learning formats (face to face, hybrid, online) ● Excellent interpersonal and team-building skills ● Demonstrated contributions in the areas of teaching, scholarship and service consistent with appointment on the educator track at the rank of Associate Professor or above on the non-tenure line (NTL). ● Applicants eligible for hire must hold primary residence in an approved state . Academic rank will be offered commensurate with education and experience. Preferred qualifications ● Additional certifications (e.g., Family Nurse Practitioner, Emergency Nurse Practitioner, Women’s Health Nurse Practitioner, Certified Nurse Midwife, Informatics) ● Expertise in a variety of teaching modalities including face to face, virtual learning, asynchronous and synchronous learning ● Demonstrated expertise in curriculum development and outcome evaluation Position responsibilities include Administration (up to 40%) ● Management, planning and coordination of all aspects of program operations including oversight of clinical education and placement . ● Ensuring integration of BSON values including but not limited to antiracism, diversity, equity, and inclusion. ● Coordination and implementation of the curriculum to reflect current practice guidelines. ● Ensures accreditation requirements are met and annual outcome reports are submitted. ● Oversees the program evaluation plan. ● Monitor and report program outcomes, i.e., retention/graduation rates, certification rates, and employment rates. ● Selection, supervision, evaluation and fostering professional development of program faculty. ● Collaboration with faculty and staff colleagues, as well as leadership to ensure outstanding program graduates and effective planning and implementation of future initiatives. ● Enhancement of the program’s reputation and visibility through engagement in relevant professional activities. Teaching (up to 50%) ● Teaches in area of expertise as needed ● Participates in curriculum and course development ● Develops and implements innovative teaching strategies Scholarship & Service (10-20%) ● Engagement in scholarly activities such as research, publications and grant writing. ● Participating in program, school, university, and other committees as required. ● Participating in all relevant program and BSON student events as required. ● Didactic and clinical instruction within areas of expertise (including, entry level, graduate-level core, and specialty courses) across multiple teaching modalities. ● Teaching at objective clinical intensives (OCI) in-person on-campus and virtually Additional responsibilities ● Faculty are expected to teach across a range of pathways (direct entry, undergraduate, graduate) and modalities to meet the needs of the school. ● Attendance at on-campus events consistent with the Faculty Handbook Faculty Roles and Responsibilities (e.g., commencement, school wide events, periodic curriculum and programming meetings) ● Faculty advising for students in precepted experiences ●      Supervision, evaluation and fostering professional development of program faculty ● Collaboration with faculty and staff colleagues, as well as leadership to ensure outstanding program graduates and effective planning/implementation of future initiatives ● Enhancement of the program’s reputation and visibility through engagement in relevant professional activities ● Participation in faculty governance, including faculty and operations meetings ● On-going professional development and participation in curricular revision and and implementation, with a focus on advancing health equity and addressing racism with the program content and, ● Participate in scholarship and service activities and maintain current clinical practice as it relates to state law for practitioners. The Berkley School of Nursing builds on a 120-year tradition of excellence in nursing education at the professional, advanced practice, and doctoral levels. Our on-campus and distance-based, online nursing programs emphasize research and scholarship, and are grounded in a social justice framework. Our commitment to nursing is infused with the Jesuit values of caring for the whole person - cura personalis - and serving the common good. To learn more about the Berkley School of Nursing, please visit nursing.georgetown.edu . To learn more about the Office of Institutional Diversity, Equity, & Affirmative Action (IDEAA) vision statement, please visit https://ideaa.georgetown.edu/vision-statement/ . As a school with a core mission of social justice and a diverse student body, the successful applicant will also have demonstrated a commitment to advancing health equity and addressing racism in graduate nursing education, including working with racialized and marginalized communities and students who hold a range of worldviews. All interested applicants are encouraged to read the GUSON Mission, Vision, Values and Commitment to Equity, Diversity, Inclusion, Belonging, Justice and Anti-Racism prior to applying. Interested applicants should submit electronically a letter of interest, a curriculum vitae, and a values statement via Interfolio. All inquiries, nominations, referrals, and materials will be maintained in confidence. Values Statement for All BSON Position Applicants At the Berkley School of Nursing (BSON) at Georgetown University, our mission, vision, and values in the Jesuit tradition are foundational to all we do. Our values bind us culturally and permeate our strategic initiatives. Please read and reflect on the BSON mission, vision, and values and provide a values statement describing how your personal and professional values align with these commitments. You may wish to include examples of how this alignment has manifested in your current or prior work. (Limit 500 words) Need Assistance If you are a qualified individual with a disability and need a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process, please click here for more information, or contact the Office of Institutional Diversity, Equity, and Affirmative Action (IDEAA) at 202-687-4798 or ideaa@georgetown.edu. Need assistance with the application process? Please call 202-687-2500. For more information about the suite of benefits, professional development and community involvement opportunities that make up Georgetown's commitment to its employees, please visit the Georgetown Works website . EEO Statement Georgetown University is an Equal Opportunity/Affirmative Action Employer fully dedicated to achieving a diverse faculty and staff.  All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, national origin, age, sex (including pregnancy, gender identity and expression, and sexual orientation), disability status, protected veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by law . Benefits Georgetown University offers a comprehensive and competitive benefit package that includes medical, dental, vision, disability and life insurance, retirement savings, tuition assistance, work-life balance benefits, employee discounts and an array of voluntary insurance options. You can learn more about benefits and eligibility on the Department of Human Resources website. T he projected salary pay range for this position is $ 120,000 – $155,000 , which represents the full range of anticipated compensation for this position. Compensation is determined by a number of factors including, but not limited to, the candidate’s individual qualifications, experience, education, skills, and certifications, as well as the University’s business needs and external factors."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195884": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor of Global Health Economics",
      "description": "Department: Georgetown University: Medical Center: School of Health: Global Health Location: Medical Center Posted: Oct 15, 2026 Deadline: Jul 01, 2027 Georgetown University invites applications for a full-time faculty position in Global Health Economics at the rank of Assistant or Associate Professor on the educator track, beginning Fall 2027. We seek a dynamic economist who is engaged with the frontier research on the economics of health around the world and who is an energetic, innovative educator. The successful candidate will anchor and expand Georgetown's teaching in health economics and global health at the undergraduate and graduate levels. Successful candidates will have an active research/policy agenda. Specific focus and sub-field are open. Areas of interest include, but are not limited to, public finance and macroeconomics of health; financing of health systems; health, growth, and economic shocks, including epidemics and climate change; health insurance and health care markets; and other areas of the cross-country and global economics of health. We are particularly interested in candidates whose work addresses the complexity and diversity of low- and middle-income countries, and who in their teaching can connect that work to high-income settings, including the US. Duties include but are not limited to: Teaching core and elective courses in health economics, health financing, and applied economic analysis Bringing current research into the classroom Developing new courses and program pathways Mentoring students Work Interactions This is a full-time, renewable appointment based in the department of Global Health in the School of Health at the Georgetown University Medical Center. Rooted in Washington, DC, the position offers a strong interdisciplinary community and proximity to the global policy ecosystem. Requirements and Qualifications Candidates must hold a PhD in economics or a closely related field by the start of the appointment. We seek research-active candidates with demonstrated excellence in teaching at the undergraduate and/or graduate level, and the ability to teach economics effectively to students from a range of disciplinary backgrounds. Preferred Qualifications Experience developing new courses or curricula Experience with active, applied, or case-based teaching methods A record of mentoring students Policy or research engagement with governments or multilateral institutions. Applicants should submit a cover letter, CV, a representative publication, teaching statement (including courses the candidate is prepared to teach and ideas for new courses), evidence of teaching effectiveness such as course evaluations or sample syllabi if available, and the names of three references. All materials must be submitted through Interfolio. Applications received by December 1, 2026 will receive full consideration. First-round interviews will be conducted virtually in December 2026 and January 2027. The projected salary pay range for this position is $120,000 – $140,000, which represents the full range of anticipated compensation for this position. Compensation is determined by a number of factors including, but not limited to, the candidate’s individual qualifications, experience, education, skills, and certifications, as well as the University’s business needs and external factors.\""
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/196155": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Fellowship in Health Economics",
      "description": "Department: Georgetown University: Main Campus: McDonough School of Business: Strategy Location: Main Campus Posted: Oct 21, 2026 Georgetown University’s McDonough School of Business invites applications for a Postdoctoral Fellowship in Health Economics. The fellow will work directly with Professor Yunan Ji . The fellowship is for one year. The fellowship may be renewed for a second year by mutual agreement, subject to funding. The position is available immediately, with a start date as soon as mutually agreeable. The postdoctoral fellow will collaborate with Professor Ji as a coauthor on grant-funded research examining provider behavior, financial incentives, and regulatory oversight in Medicare, using Medicare claims and other administrative data. The ideal candidate is a researcher whose interests align closely with these themes and who will bring independent expertise to the development of the research. Further project details will be shared during the interview process. ABOUT GEORGETOWN UNIVERSITY’s MCDONOUGH SCHOOL OF BUSINESS Georgetown University’s McDonough School of Business offers unparalleled access to the world's business, policy, and thought leaders. The school, which attracts faculty and students from around the world, is committed to building global business knowledge and inspiring ethical leaders to serve the common good. Known for its emphasis at the intersection of business and global affairs and its values-based approach to business as part of Georgetown's rich Catholic, Jesuit tradition, the school is also focused on how business can be a force for change by addressing fields of the future, such as healthcare, sustainability, and AI and the future of work. The school's undergraduate and graduate degree programs provide solid grounding in all the core management disciplines, with an emphasis on the global, ethical, and political environment of business. The McDonough School of Business has an ongoing commitment to being a community in diversity as a fundamental aspect of the Georgetown community. The projected annual salary range for this position is $80,000–$95,000. Compensation is determined by a number of factors including, but not limited to, the candidate’s individual qualifications, experience, education, skills, and certifications, as well as the University’s business needs and external factors. A Ph.D. in economics or a related field by the appointment start date (required). Candidates expecting to complete their Ph.D. by August 2027 are welcome to apply. Prior experience with Medicare or other health care claims data (strongly preferred) Applications should be submitted electronically via Interfolio. Applications will be considered on a rolling basis. Materials Required for Application: 1) A cover letter describing your research agenda and its alignment with the research themes described above. Your anticipated start date and the end date through which you would be available for this position 2) A current CV 3) Graduate transcripts (unofficial copies are acceptable) 4) One or more original research papers 5) Three letters of reference submitted directly via Interfolio"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195977": {
      "title": "Assistant Teaching Professor, Program on Justice and Peace Studies",
      "description": "Department: Georgetown University: Main Campus: College of Arts & Sciences: Justice and Peace Studies Location: Main Campus Posted: Oct 22, 2026 Deadline: Dec 10, 2027 The Program on Justice and Peace Studies (JUPS) at Georgetown University invites applications for a three-year, non-tenure-line faculty position at the rank of Assistant Teaching Professor, with the possibility of renewal. The position will begin in August 2027. JUPS is a thriving, interdisciplinary undergraduate program offering a Major and a Minor open to all students at Georgetown University. Teaching faculty maintain a 3-3 teaching load (three courses, or 9 credit hours, per semester) at the undergraduate level for a total of 6 courses per academic year. This is a teaching position, and candidates must show a strong promise of excellence in teaching. The successful candidate will support the program’s core curriculum by teaching a selection of our foundational undergraduate courses, which include Introduction to Justice and Peace Studies, Nonviolence Theory and Practice, Peacebuilding and Conflict Transformation. Additional responsibilities include course planning, curricular development, undergraduate student advising and mentorship, service, and active participation in the broader JUPS program community. Qualifications A Ph.D. in a relevant discipline (such as Justice, Peace and Conflict Studies, Sociology, Philosophy or Political Science) is required prior to the appointment start date. Candidates must possess a teaching and research profile focused on fields such as Law and Civil Rights, History and Dynamics of Nonviolence, Resistance, Social movements, Philosophy and Theories of Liberation, decolonial studies and freedom studies. Application Instructions Click on Apply Now to submit the following required materials: 1. Cover Letter detailing the fit for the JUPS program 2. Curriculum Vitae (CV) 3. Evidence of teaching effectiveness (including recent course evaluations and sample syllabi) 4. A list of 3 references Applications are due by Dec 10th. For questions related to this position, please contact Dr. Elham Atashi, Director, ea543@georgetown.edu The projected base salary range is $55,112-$71,000 . Compensation is determined by a number of factors including, but not limited to, the candidate’s individual qualifications, experience, education, skills, and certifications, as well as the University’s business needs and external factors."
    },
    "https://georgetown.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/Georgetown_Admin_Careers/job/Law-Center/Program---Ops-Assistant_JR27970": {
      "title": "Program & Ops Assistant",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-26\nFull time\n\nGeorgetown University comprises two unique campuses in the nation’s capital. With the Hilltop Campus located in the heart of the historic Georgetown neighborhood, and the Capitol Campus, just minutes from the U.S. Capitol and U.S. Supreme Court, Georgetown University offers rigorous academic programs, a global perspective, and unparalleled opportunities to engage with Washington, D.C. Our community is a close-knit group of remarkable individuals driven by intellectual inquiry, a commitment to social justice, and a shared dedication to making a difference in the world. Requirements Job Overview The Georgetown Center for the Constitution (GCC), founded in 2012, offers a variety of programs on constitutional law and theory at Georgetown Law, placing special emphasis on how best to remain faithful to the Constitution's text. Led by Faculty Directors Randy Barnett and Stephanie Barclay, the Center organizes numerous programs on constitutional law and theory. All of the Center's activities are designed to engage scholars, students, and Supreme Court justices in conversations about how to interpret and apply the document that sits under glass less than ten blocks from Georgetown Law. This hybrid role is central to the smooth operation of the Center. A primary focus of this position is providing comprehensive executive, administrative, and operational support to the Executive Director and Randy Barnett and Stephanie Barclay in their roles as Faculty at Georgetown and Faculty Directors of the Center. Duties include managing complex executive calendars, coordinating travel, assisting with law school classes, course materials, syllabus preparation, exam logistics, academic research projects, and handling general administrative needs as directed. The Assistant also serves as the crucial link across our operations, communications, and programming efforts. The Assistant leads the Center's day-to-day communications—taking primary ownership of social media management, designing and distributing email newsletters, and overseeing digital outreach—while providing key workflow support to the Program Manager and Operations & Project Manager on event execution, financial processing, and general Center administration. The ideal candidate is a highly organized, proactive, and detail-oriented individual with a strong interest in constitutional law. Work Interactions The Program and Operations Assistant reports directly to the Executive Director and works closely with the Program Manager, Operations & Project Manager, and Faculty Directors on Center tasks. This position receives daily workflow direction from both the Operations & Project Manager and Program Manager, assisting with executive, administrative, financial, logistical, programmatic, coursework, communications, and day-to-day operational duties.   Requirements and Qualifications Bachelor's degree is required. 1-3 years of relevant administrative or program support experience, preferably in an academic, non-profit, or legal setting. Internships and related experience will be considered. Demonstrated ability to manage multiple projects simultaneously with meticulous attention to detail. Excellent written and verbal communication abilities, with the capacity to interact professionally with faculty, students, staff, and distinguished guests Experience with digital media and social media platforms is strongly desired. High level of proficiency with Microsoft Office Suite, Google Workspace, email marketing software (e.g., Mailchimp), and social media platforms Experience with WordPress is preferred, but a demonstrated ability to learn new programs quickly is essential. An ability to produce and edit podcasts is a plus. A self-starter who can work independently but also thrives as part of a small, collaborative team. A bility to handle confidential information with the utmost discretion. Work Mode Designation This position has been designated as hybrid remote 1 day a week. Please note that work mode designations are regularly reviewed in order to meet the evolving needs of the University. Such review may necessitate a change to a position’s mode of work designation. Complete details about Georgetown University’s mode of work designations for staff and AAP positions can be found on the Department of Human Resources website: https://hr.georgetown.edu/mode-of-work-designation. Applying for this Position To apply for this position, please use the Georgetown hiring platform and include the following two documents: Resume / CV Cover Letter   Your cover letter must explicitly answer all of the following questions (bullet-point form preferred): Why are you interested in this position? What experience do you bring to this role? How does this role fit into your five-year career plan? Where do you want to see yourself in 10 years? Benefits and Professional Development This position is eligible for Georgetown University's comprehensive benefits package. A key highlight is the Tuition Assistance Program (TAP), which provides employees with a 90% tuition benefit for graduate programs after one full year of service. This benefit can be applied to degree programs at Georgetown, including the J.D. program at the Law Center, making this an outstanding opportunity for individuals planning to attend Georgetown Law while continuing in this role or advancing to the Program Manager position.   Pay Range The projected pay range for this full-time, non-exempt position is $19.71 – $28.85 per hour / $41,000 – $60,000 annually (with a target hiring range of $24.04 – $26.44 per hour / $50,000 – $55,000 annually). Compensation is determined by a number of factors including, but not limited to, the candidate’s individual qualifications, experience, education, skills, and certifications, as well as the University’s business needs and external factors. Pay Range: The projected salary or hourly pay range for this position which represents the full range of anticipated compensation is: $20.16 - $31.30 Compensation is determined by a number of factors including, but not limited to, the candidate’s individual qualifications, experience, education, skills, and certifications, as well as the University’s business needs and external factors. Current Georgetown Employees: If you currently work at Georgetown University, please exit this website and login to GMS ( gms.georgetown.edu ) using your Net ID and password. Then select the Career worklet on your GMS Home dashboard to view Jobs at Georgetown. Submission Guidelines: Please note that in order to be considered an applicant for any position at Georgetown University you must submit a resume for each position of interest for which you believe you are qualified. Documents are not kept on file for future positions. Need Assistance: If you are a qualified individual with a disability and need a reasonable accommodations for any part of the application and hiring process, please visit the Office for Equal Opportunity Compliance website for general information about requesting accommodations , as well as information about requesting accommodations specifically for applicants . You also can also contact the Office for Equal Opportunity Compliance at (202) 687-4798 or oeoc@georgetown.edu . Need some assistance with the application process? Please call 202-687-2500. For more information about the suite of benefits, professional development and community involvement opportunities that make up Georgetown's commitment to its employees, please visit the Georgetown Works website . EEO Statement: GU is an Equal Opportunity Employer . All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply, and will receive consideration for employment without regard to age, citizenship, color, disability, family responsibilities, gender identity and expression, genetic information, marital status, matriculation, national origin, race, religion, personal appearance, political affiliation, sex, sexual orientation, veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by law . Benefits: Georgetown University offers a comprehensive and competitive benefit package that includes medical, dental, vision, disability and life insurance, retirement savings, tuition assistance, work-life balance benefits, employee discounts and an array of voluntary insurance options. You can learn more about benefits and eligibility on the Department of Human Resources website ."
