    "https://georgetown.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/Georgetown_Admin_Careers/job/Medical-Center/Research-Assistant_JR27965": {
      "title": "Research Assistant",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-29\nFull time\n\nGeorgetown University comprises two unique campuses in the nation’s capital. With the Hilltop Campus located in the heart of the historic Georgetown neighborhood, and the Capitol Campus, just minutes from the U.S. Capitol and U.S. Supreme Court, Georgetown University offers rigorous academic programs, a global perspective, and unparalleled opportunities to engage with Washington, D.C. Our community is a close-knit group of remarkable individuals driven by intellectual inquiry, a commitment to social justice, and a shared dedication to making a difference in the world. Requirements Job Overview The Research Assistant, Center for Health Equity – Georgetown University, provides assistance for faculty and learner research projects and supports research infrastructure and capacity building activities in the Center for Health Equity and for the Department of Family Medicine.  Research Assistant will serve as the primary project manager for multiple on-going research projects in all phases of development and implementation, including supervision of work-study students and medical school, graduate and undergraduate student interns. Additionally, the position will contribute to research design, IRB applications and processing, data analysis, and dissemination of study results. The position directly supports the Center for Health Equity Advisory Committee and subcommittees, and Area Health Education Center Advisory Committee and subcommittees for scheduling, event planning, meeting agendas and minutes, annual and periodic reports, website and newsletter updates and dissemination, and subcommittee specific assignments as assigned by the Director and chairs. The Research Assistant supports the center director, center grant-related Principal Investigators, CAPRICORN Director, and Department Administrator. This role will serve in a collaborative team support capacity for the department’s administrative team of Administrators, Program Managers, Research Assistants/Associates, Research Faculty, Research Fellows, graduate students, and their respective divisions.   Additional duties include, but are not limited to:   Oversee and assist in ongoing studies, including research design, data management, study materials development, data analysis, writing and editing scientific manuscripts for publication Responsible for institutional review board (IRB) submissions and serve as the primary regulatory coordinator for IRB-related communications for the research division Help department faculty members submit and communicate with the IRB Supervise and train research assistants, as well as medical school, graduate and undergraduate student interns Responsible for collecting and analyzing evaluative data for residency program on resident performance, patient satisfaction, or any grant-related indicators. Develop, edit and prepare grant proposals, study protocols and supporting documentation for federal, foundation and private industry-sponsored research funding agencies Responsible for coordination and submission of pre-award grant applications and reports with university and extramural research administrative offices, subcontractors, subawardee investigators, and consultants Provide support and assistance for researchers, research infrastructure, financial management functions and capacity building activities for the Center for Health Equity and the Department of Family Responsible for institutional review board (IRB) submissions and serve as the primary regulatory coordinator for IRB-related communications for the research division Help department faculty members submit and communicate with the IRB Supervise and train research assistants, as well as medical school, graduate and undergraduate student interns Coordinate research activities, including health-services research, medical education research and CAPRICORN, a practice-based research network (PBRN) of primary care providers in the DC metropolitan area Coordinate survey instruments and statistical programs for study usage Support all meetings and events, and present opportunities for recruitment and enrollment in practice-based research to faculty, residents, and staff Support and manage communications for the Scholarly Activity Working Group, department, Center for Health Equity for upcoming grants, reports, projects, meetings and events Assist CAPRICORN Director, and study investigators in developing and implementing project protocols and recruitment plans for subject and student involvement Oversee and assist with medical, graduate and undergraduate student independent-study or other learner research projects, short and long term. Prepare study budgets with the help of PI, department administrator, in accordance with federal, sponsor and institutional policies, procedures and guidelinesMonitor budgets and grant-related and research expenses, and reconcile expense reports with department administratorManage study participant payments. Complete sponsor annual progress reports and assist with financial reports.Educational/research data entry and analysis Coordinate grant-related subcontractor, independent consultant and other outside vendor invoices, payments and contracts with the department and university administrative offices Procure and manage inventory of research, laboratory, and other general office supplies with the support of the department administrative officer Coordinate subcontractor, independent consultant and other outside vendor invoices, payments and contracts with the department and university administrative offices Work Interactions Reports to Center Director: Reports to Center and Department Administrator Requirements and Qualifications Bachelor’s degree in health or science related field; Master’s degree preferred. Certified Research Administration designation Preferred minimum of 1 year of research experience as a student including coursework, extramural research programs or research administration Required to maintain appropriate Collaborative Institutional Training Initiative (CITI) training according to the supported research projects and IRB protocols. Knowledge of research methods, human research subjects protection and Good Clinical Practices, FDA regulations, OMB Uniform Guidance and NIH funding guidelines. Analytical background; strong communication, writing and editing skills; excellent organizational skills; detail-oriented Knowledge of grants.gov, REDCap, SPSS or other statistical programs, Endnotes, Word, Excel   Work Mode Designation This position has been designated as On Campus. Please note that work mode designations are regularly reviewed in order to meet the evolving needs of the University.  Such review may necessitate a change to a position’s mode of work designation. Complete details about Georgetown University’s mode of work designations for staff positions can be found on the Department of Human Resources website: https://hr.georgetown.edu/mode-of-work-designation .     Pay Range: The projected salary or hourly pay range for this position which represents the full range of anticipated compensation is: $41,926.00 - $65,090.00 Compensation is determined by a number of factors including, but not limited to, the candidate’s individual qualifications, experience, education, skills, and certifications, as well as the University’s business needs and external factors. Current Georgetown Employees: If you currently work at Georgetown University, please exit this website and login to GMS ( gms.georgetown.edu ) using your Net ID and password. Then select the Career worklet on your GMS Home dashboard to view Jobs at Georgetown. Submission Guidelines: Please note that in order to be considered an applicant for any position at Georgetown University you must submit a resume for each position of interest for which you believe you are qualified. Documents are not kept on file for future positions. Need Assistance: If you are a qualified individual with a disability and need a reasonable accommodations for any part of the application and hiring process, please visit the Office for Equal Opportunity Compliance website for general information about requesting accommodations , as well as information about requesting accommodations specifically for applicants . You also can also contact the Office for Equal Opportunity Compliance at (202) 687-4798 or oeoc@georgetown.edu . Need some assistance with the application process? Please call 202-687-2500. For more information about the suite of benefits, professional development and community involvement opportunities that make up Georgetown's commitment to its employees, please visit the Georgetown Works website . EEO Statement: GU is an Equal Opportunity Employer . All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply, and will receive consideration for employment without regard to age, citizenship, color, disability, family responsibilities, gender identity and expression, genetic information, marital status, matriculation, national origin, race, religion, personal appearance, political affiliation, sex, sexual orientation, veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by law . Benefits: Georgetown University offers a comprehensive and competitive benefit package that includes medical, dental, vision, disability and life insurance, retirement savings, tuition assistance, work-life balance benefits, employee discounts and an array of voluntary insurance options. You can learn more about benefits and eligibility on the Department of Human Resources website ."
    },
    "https://georgetown.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/Georgetown_Admin_Careers/job/Main-Campus/Director-of-Partnerships_JR27976": {
      "title": "Director of Partnerships",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-28\nFull time\n\nGeorgetown University comprises two unique campuses in the nation’s capital. With the Hilltop Campus located in the heart of the historic Georgetown neighborhood, and the Capitol Campus, just minutes from the U.S. Capitol and U.S. Supreme Court, Georgetown University offers rigorous academic programs, a global perspective, and unparalleled opportunities to engage with Washington, D.C. Our community is a close-knit group of remarkable individuals driven by intellectual inquiry, a commitment to social justice, and a shared dedication to making a difference in the world. Requirements Job Overview Director of Partnerships, Emerging Studies Institute- Georgetown University The Emerging Studies Institute (ESI) is a new dynamic and important component of the Georgetown University infrastructure tasked with the development and delivery of a range of degree and non degree programs. The Institute will provide an opportunity for faculty  to create exciting new programs involving inter and intra disciplinary collaboration. ESI will help develop the programs and identify the clients and external audiences for their delivery via market research and marketing. These programs have the capacity to generate important funds to support the mission of the associated units and departments. Some of the funds can be employed as financial incentives to engage faculty .   Custom and Executive Education programs are included as part of this initiative and creating and maintaining a marketable portfolio of programs is a key part of the ESI’s strategic growth plan.  However, while these programs are potentially lucrative, it should be  acknowledged that it is a competitive space and decisions regarding the areas in which we develop programs must be well informed. Moreover, we have to devote resources to identify and attract clients and audiences.  As this is a relatively unexplored market, one of ESI’s first and most important objectives is to create a client base and for which we will develop a portfolio of educational products for single or multiple delivery. The Director will be tasked with the critical functions of attracting audiences for these new programs and assisting the ESI director in determining which areas of study the ESI should invest funds to develop new courses.   The Director of Partnerships responsibilities will include extensive management of people, processes, and client interaction daily across cultures and borders, supervising the communications and interactions with hundreds of participants across multiple programs. They will also entail  detailed coordination with faculty members from all university units and centers, internal stakeholders, external vendors, and contractors.  The D.P. will also be responsible for identifying potential clients and establishing agreements for faculty to deliver programs. Moreover, the D.P. will be responsible for maintaining portfolios of programs and clients and ensuring a steady flow of activity. This position will require flexibility as the incumbent may travel up to 10% of the year domestically and abroad and work some weekends and evenings up to 20% of the year for program deliveries and events. The  Director of Partnerships has responsibilities that include but are not limited to: Lead a team of 3 direct reports and  2 student workers Minimum target of of 5 programs with an associated  minimum revenue target of $1mmin the upcoming year Work Interactions   Reporting to the Director of the Emerging Studies Institute, the Director of Partnerships takes a key leadership role in managing the delivery of a large portfolio of  Executive Custom Programs, and hundreds of client relationships at Georgetown’s Emerging Studies Institute. They have responsibilities that include developing strategic goals for business growth, building and maintaining a high-performing team under pressure, project coordination and collaboration, engaging in extensive client interaction and program delivery – not only onsite at Georgetown, but also offsite, as well as domestically, internationally, and virtually. Student workers will be a direct report. Program coordinators will be an indirect report.     Requirements and Qualifications Master`s degree in a related field of business 10 or more years of experience in client relationship management or sales Demonstrated leadership skills with the ability to lead presentations, meetings, and public speaking Professional maturity and judgment to handle complex pressure and constraints at highest ethical standards Global citizen with skilled intercultural communication skills to engage and adapt to multicultural stakeholders Outstanding organizational abilities, a critical eye for detail, and outstanding written and oral communications Leadership experience in managing a marketing or sales campaign Ability to long-term plan, make decisions and act under pressure Ability to build high-performing teams by recruiting, hiring, and developing strong talent Skilled with team management and the balance of delegating to others and leaning in where needed Demonstrated experience in designing and managing multiple concurrent projects seamlessly Demonstrated experience working in teams and managing student workers and junior staff Availability to work some weekends and evenings up to 20% for client/team events and some offsite travel       Work Mode Designation This position’s work mode has been designated as Hybrid. Please note that work mode designations are regularly reviewed to meet the evolving needs of the University.  Such review may necessitate a change to a position’s mode of work designation. Complete details about Georgetown University’s mode of work designations can be found on the Department of Human Resources website: https://hr.georgetown.edu/mode-of-work-designation .     Pay Range: The projected salary or hourly pay range for this position which represents the full range of anticipated compensation is: $103,723.00 - $202,778.93 Compensation is determined by a number of factors including, but not limited to, the candidate’s individual qualifications, experience, education, skills, and certifications, as well as the University’s business needs and external factors. Current Georgetown Employees: If you currently work at Georgetown University, please exit this website and login to GMS ( gms.georgetown.edu ) using your Net ID and password. Then select the Career worklet on your GMS Home dashboard to view Jobs at Georgetown. Submission Guidelines: Please note that in order to be considered an applicant for any position at Georgetown University you must submit a resume for each position of interest for which you believe you are qualified. Documents are not kept on file for future positions. Need Assistance: If you are a qualified individual with a disability and need a reasonable accommodations for any part of the application and hiring process, please visit the Office for Equal Opportunity Compliance website for general information about requesting accommodations , as well as information about requesting accommodations specifically for applicants . You also can also contact the Office for Equal Opportunity Compliance at (202) 687-4798 or oeoc@georgetown.edu . Need some assistance with the application process? Please call 202-687-2500. For more information about the suite of benefits, professional development and community involvement opportunities that make up Georgetown's commitment to its employees, please visit the Georgetown Works website . EEO Statement: GU is an Equal Opportunity Employer . All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply, and will receive consideration for employment without regard to age, citizenship, color, disability, family responsibilities, gender identity and expression, genetic information, marital status, matriculation, national origin, race, religion, personal appearance, political affiliation, sex, sexual orientation, veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by law . Benefits: Georgetown University offers a comprehensive and competitive benefit package that includes medical, dental, vision, disability and life insurance, retirement savings, tuition assistance, work-life balance benefits, employee discounts and an array of voluntary insurance options. You can learn more about benefits and eligibility on the Department of Human Resources website ."
    }
